Modern JVMs use JIT (Just-In-Time) Compilation.

Instead of translating every instruction repeatedly, the JIT compiles frequently executed ("hot") code into optimized native machine code for better performance.


Bytecode? -->  Platform-independent intermediate code generated by the Java compiler (javac) from Java source code. It is stored in .class files and executed by the JVM.


public class Hello {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        System.out.println("Hello World");

    }

}
public ---> access modifier accessible from everywhere 
static --->static method belongs to the class, not to an object , no need to create object JVM can call it using method name , it gets loaded into memory at compile time
void ---> return type , means return nothing
main ---> standard method name which JVM calls first
String[] args----> command line argument Java Hello arg1 arg2 arg3 arg4 ---> we can access them using index which starts from 0
System ---> predefined Class in java lang package
out --->public static field inside system class ,"Give me the standard output stream."
println() is a method of PrintStream



